"info\n  this image      00200000 to 0081AB73   6400884 bytes, measured from the image itself\n  reserved        00200000 to 008FFFFF   this monitor's code, measured from the image\n  reserved        0D000000 to 0F6FFFFF   its variables, placed by --bss-addr\n  reserved        001FF000 to 001FFFFF   the autoboot record\n  reserved        08A00000 to 091FFFFF   the DSI framebuffer\n  reserved        001FC000 to 001FEFFF   the reserved raw-secondary stacks\n  reserved        09200000 to 099FFFFF   the driver-module arena and its staging window\n  reserved        001FB000 to 001FBFFF   the boot phase record\n  reserved        0B000000 to 0BBFFFFF   the screenshot capture area\n  reserved        10000000 to 13FFFFFF   the reserved Vulkan device-memory heap\n  its stack top   00100000               and it grows downwards from there\n  stage a file at 00900000               which is what the upload tools ask for\n            A write into any of that is refused and the refusal names\n            the region. The first line is MEASURED, not declared: the\n            image carries the address of its own end, so the monitor\n            reserves exactly what it is and there is no maximum size.\n            Type map for the same numbers with the payload windows.\naddresses   every address that exists on this board answers SAFE, so\n            the commands that take an address from you are refused by\n            nothing but the monitor's own window guards. Nothing here\n            is known to stall the bus: the peripheral window is\n            clocked by the firmware before this monitor is entered,\n            and an address with nothing behind it reads a value\n            rather than hanging. Anvil asks the question because its\n            other target is not like this - there, one read of a\n            gated block stops the processor until the power is\n            pulled - and one core asks every board the same thing.\nmemory top  two things are refused here and they are different. An\n            address past the memory this board has, but below the\n            peripheral aperture, reads a plausible value rather than\n            hanging - a wrong answer that looks like a right one is\n            worse than a stop - so it is refused with the size named.\n            And EVERYTHING ABOVE FOUR GIGABYTES except the PCI\n            Express window is refused for a harder reason: this\n            monitor's page tables map nothing there, so an access is\n            a translation fault and Anvil stops on it. Memory does\n            resume up there on a 4 or 8 GB board and this monitor\n            still cannot reach it. force overrides either refusal for\n            one address and one command line, as it does any other -\n            and forcing the second one will stop the board.\nlevel       exception level 3, the secure monitor level. Our own arm stub\n            put us here and did not drop to level 2, so the ARM side of\n            this machine is entirely ours: SCR_EL3, the secure GIC banks\n            and the translation regime below all belong to this monitor.\n            Remove the armstub line from CONFIG.TXT and reset to go back\n            to level 2.\n            SCTLR_EL3 = $0000000030C51835, which is NOT what the stub left. Something\n            has written it since - `cache` says whether that was us.\nboard       Pi 4 Model B, BCM2711, 4 GB, rev 1.2   (revision code $00C03112)\nRAM         4 GB, from the board revision code the firmware reports.\n            An address past it is refused, naming that size. Memory\n            runs from zero and STOPS at the peripheral aperture;\n            what is left of that figure lives above four gigabytes\n            and THIS MONITOR DOES NOT MAP IT - reading up there is\n            a translation fault that stops the board, so it is\n            refused as well, and for that reason rather than for\n            the size. On a 4 GB board that is the last 64 MB.\ntemperature 115.1 F (46 C, 46214 millidegrees from the firmware) inside the chip, and the firmware\n            starts slowing the board down at 185.0 F.\nclock rates. The configured rate is what the firmware intends and the\nmeasured rate is what it observes; the governor moves the two apart\nwithout telling anybody, so both are shown.\n  the processor cores       configured 1500000000 Hz, measured 1500398464 Hz\n  the VideoCore and buses   configured 500000000 Hz, measured 500000992 Hz\n  the SD card interface     configured 250000000 Hz, measured 250000496 Hz\n  the serial port           configured 48000000 Hz, measured 48001464 Hz\n  the tick counter          54000000 Hz\nEvery timeout in this monitor is counted on that last one, and it runs\nat its own fixed rate no matter what the processor clock is doing. If a\ntransfer gives up far too early, that is the number to look at first.\ni2c bus.\nThe I2C bus 1 clock is about 333333 Hz.\nThe divider is fed from 499987808 Hz, which is\n  the VideoCore core clock, measured by the firmware just now.\nFrom that clock this bus can be set anywhere from 7629 Hz\nto 250000496 Hz, and nothing outside that will be accepted.\nThe bus runs on GPIO 2 as SDA and GPIO 3 as SCL, which the controller can only\nreach while both are on alt0.\nRight now GPIO 2 is NOT on that function and GPIO 3 is NOT on that function.\nAt the pads SDA reads high and SCL reads high.\nAn idle I2C bus rests high on both lines, which is what that is.\npmf> "